  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "The Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process is an extension of the Brownian-motion model to include a linear restoring force back to the origin. The one-dimensional version of this diffusion process for $ -\\infty < x < \\infty $ is given by $$ m(x)=-ax\\quad and\\quad v(x)=b $$ with a, b > 0. As with Brownian motion, the distribution of $ x_{t} $ (given the starting condition $ x_{0} $) is also normal, but now with a mean and a variance of $$ \\mu_{t}=x_{0}e^{-at}\\quad and\\quad\\sigma_{t}^{2}=\\frac{b}{2a}\\left(1-e^{-2at}\\right) $$",
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix1:p017:b3",
        "Evolution_appendix1:p017:b5",
        "Evolution_appendix1:p017:b4",
        "Evolution_appendix1:p017:b7",
        "Evolution_appendix1:p017:b8"
      ],
